The '''Ethiopian''', '''Aethiopian''', '''Æthiopian''', '''Æthiopic''' '''Ocean''' or '''Sea''' ({{langx|la|Æthiopicum Mare}} or ''Oceanus Æthiopicus''; {{langx|ar|بحر الحبش|Bahr al-Habash}}; {{Langx|mao|Axúbi akaraw}}) is the second-largest (after the [[Shendan Ocean|Shendan]]) of Adonia's six [[Borders of the oceans|oceanic divisions]], and, with a total area of about 92 million km², it covers approximately 27.3 percent of Adonia's ocean surface area. 

Through its separation of [[Kafto-Illypnifosia]] from [[Lurando-Abyala]], the Ethiopian has played a central role in the development of human society, globalization, and the histories of many nations.

== Etymology ==
The [[W:Greek language|Aetolian]] name ''Aithiopia'' ({{Langx|el|Αἰθιοπία|label=none}}, from {{Langx|grc|Αἰθίοψ|translit=Aithíops|lit=|label=none}}) is a [[W:Compound (linguistics)|compound]] derived of two Aetolian words: {{Langx|grc|αἴθω|translit=aíthō|lit=I burn|label=none}} + {{Langx|grc|ὤψ|translit=ṓps|lit=face|label=none}}. According to linguists, this designation properly translates in [[W:Noun|noun form]] as ''burnt-face'' and in [[W:Adjective|adjectival form]] as ''red-brown''. As such, it was used as a vague term for darker skinned populations than the Aetolians, and the term was applied to such peoples within the range of observation of the [[List of Dindaric geographers|ancient geographers]], primarily in [[South Kaftia]]. With the expansion of geographical knowledge, the [[W:Exonym and endonym|exonym]] successively extended north towards [[Central Kaftia]], crossing into the region know know as [[the Sudan]]. 

